title: Early American Baptists – Establishment, Disestablishment and Soul Liberty
description: Three answers to the question of church and state in early America, the state church, a middle way, and Baptist soul liberty, told through Roger Williams, Obadiah Holmes, Isaac Backus and John Leland.
output: lessons/history/early-american-baptists.html

# Built from the Early American Baptists outline, with the Obadiah Holmes and Daniel Merrill studies.
# Dates and names are the ones history records plainly; where the record is a tradition, the slide says so.

=== Introduction
--- Early American Baptists [title]
overline: Baptist history
heading: Early American Baptists
sub: Establishment, disestablishment and soul liberty: three answers to the question of who rules the church.
refs: Acts 5:29; Galatians 5:1
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- Three answers
lead: Early America asked one question: what should the state do about religion? There were three answers.
panel-warn: 1. Establishment | The state backs the church with taxes and laws. One church (Cotton, the Mathers, the Anglican South), or all the Christian churches alike (Patrick Henry’s 1784 bill, Massachusetts after 1780).
panel: 2. Disestablishment, the middle way | No tax-funded church, but the laws, oaths and proclamations keep the state friendly to Protestant Christianity. John Jay.
panel: 3. Soul liberty | No state support and no state control at all. A church lives on the gifts of its members, and a man’s conscience is his own. The Baptists.
callout: The Baptists fought the first answer and then pressed against the second. A friendly state, they said, is still the state in the church.

--- The long word
lead: “Antidisestablishmentarianism” comes from the nineteenth-century British fight over whether to disestablish the Church of England, and it means holding that the state church should stay.
note: In early America the word fits the first camp. The second camp wanted disestablishment, but not the Baptists’ kind.

--- What they were fighting
panel-warn: Tax-funded churches | Everyone paid for the Congregational church in New England and the Anglican church in the South, whatever he believed.
panel-warn: Licenses to preach | A man could not preach without the state church’s permission, and unlicensed preaching was a crime.
panel-warn: Infant baptism as a civil duty | Babies were baptized into the church and the commonwealth together, and refusing it marked a man as a troublemaker.
panel-warn: Enforced conformity | Fines, whippings, jail and banishment for those who would not conform.

--- What they argued for
tile: 1 | **Soul liberty.** Every man answers to God for himself. [[Romans 14:12]]
tile: 2 | **Voluntary membership.** A church is the saved, baptized upon their own profession of faith. [[Acts 8:36-37]]
tile: 3 | **No coercion in religion.** Faith cannot be forced, and the sword is not the church’s weapon. [[2 Corinthians 10:4]]
tile: 4 | **Liberty for all.** Not only for Baptists, but for every conscience, even those they disagreed with.

=== Why they refused
--- Render unto Caesar
big: Matthew 22:21 | mark: Render therefore unto Caesar the things which are Caesar’s; and unto God the things that are God’s
p: Two realms, and two kinds of authority. The magistrate keeps the peace. He does not keep the soul. [[John 18:36]] [[Romans 13:1-4]]

--- Let both grow together
scripture: Matthew 13:24-30 | mark: Let both grow together until the harvest
p: Roger Williams leaned on this parable. If the tares are among the wheat until the harvest, then the sword has no business sorting them now. [[Luke 9:54-56]]

--- We ought to obey God
big: Acts 5:29 | mark: We ought to obey God rather than men
p: The answer every one of these men gave when the court told them to stop preaching, stop baptizing, or stop meeting. [[Acts 4:19-20]]

=== Before America
--- Thomas Helwys
lead: The Baptist argument for soul liberty began in England, before any Baptist reached America.
step: 1609 | John Smyth forms a Baptist congregation of English exiles in Amsterdam, and Thomas Helwys is among them. [[Acts 8:36-37]]
step: c. 1612 | Helwys leads part of the congregation home to England, and publishes *A Short Declaration of the Mystery of Iniquity*, a plea for liberty of conscience that may be the first in the English language.
step: 1612 → | He sends a copy to King James I. Helwys and others are thrown into Newgate prison, and he probably died there about 1616.
quote: Let them be heretics, Turks, Jews, or whatsoever, it appertains not to the earthly power to punish them in the least measure. | Thomas Helwys, 1612
note: The same conviction, a century and a half earlier, that Holmes, Backus and Leland would suffer for.

=== New England
--- The New England core
tile: 1636 | **Roger Williams** banished from Massachusetts Bay; founds Providence.
tile: 1639–40s | **Providence and Newport.** The first Baptist churches in America take form.
tile: 1651 | **Obadiah Holmes** whipped on Boston Common.
tile: 1663 | **Rhode Island’s charter** guarantees religious freedom.
tile: 1770s | **Isaac Backus** carries the fight against the tax-supported church.

--- Roger Williams
step: 1635 | The Massachusetts Bay court banishes him for “newe and dangerous opinions”, chiefly that the magistrate has no authority over the soul. He flees into the winter wilderness in January 1636.
step: 1636 | He founds **Providence**, a colony where no man would be compelled to worship.
step: 1639 | Baptized, he helps form the Providence church, often counted the first Baptist church in America. He left it within months and ended as a “Seeker”, so we claim his argument and not his later path.
step: 1644 | *The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution* argues that forcing conscience is itself the sin. John Cotton of Boston answers him, and the debate runs for years.

--- John Clarke
step: 1640s | A physician, he helps found Newport and gathers its Baptist church. Sources date it anywhere from 1638 to 1648; it is among the first two Baptist churches in America and still stands.
step: 1651 | Arrested at Lynn with Obadiah Holmes and John Crandall for holding a private worship service, and imprisoned in Boston. Clarke is fined £20, Crandall £5 and Holmes £30.
step: 1652 | Publishes *Ill Newes from New England* in London, exposing Massachusetts to the English public.
step: 1663 | After years in England as agent, he secures Rhode Island’s royal charter from Charles II: “a lively experiment” in full liberty in religious concernments.

--- Obadiah Holmes
lead: Born in Lancashire about 1610, a weaver and glassmaker, Holmes left Salem and then Rehoboth for his Baptist convictions, was excommunicated, and fled to Newport in 1650.
step: July 1651 | Arrested at the home of blind William Witter in Lynn for preaching, baptizing and the Lord’s Supper.
step: Sept. 5, 1651 | Thirty lashes with a three-corded whip on Boston Common. He declined wine beforehand and preached through the whipping.
step: 1652 → | Succeeds Clarke as pastor of the Newport church, and serves it for some thirty years until his death in 1682.
quote: You have struck me as with roses. | Obadiah Holmes, to the magistrates, after the whipping
read: obadiah-holmes | Read the full study: Obadiah Holmes

--- Holmes and the apostles
big: Acts 5:41 | mark: rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer shame for his name
p: Holmes read his stripes in the light of Peter and John. He prayed that the sin not be laid to the charge of his persecutors, as Stephen did. [[Acts 7:60]] [[Galatians 6:17]]

--- Isaac Backus
step: 1724–1806 | Converted in the Great Awakening, he left the established church, became a Baptist in 1751, and pastored at Middleborough, Massachusetts, for fifty years.
step: 1768–71 | Baptists at Ashfield have their property seized to pay the Congregational minister’s tax. They petition the General Court and carry the case to the King in Council, which disallows the law. Backus takes up their cause.
step: 1773 | *An Appeal to the Public for Religious Liberty*, the plainest Baptist argument against a state-taxed church.
step: 1774 | Goes to Philadelphia with James Manning and meets the Massachusetts delegates to the First Continental Congress, to ask relief for Baptists taxed for ministers they did not choose.
p: Backus refused to take out the “certificates” that excused Baptists from the tax, because to ask the state’s permission was to grant its authority. He also wrote the history of New England Baptists.

--- Hezekiah Smith and the Warren Association
step: 1764 | Baptists found Rhode Island College, later Brown University. Hezekiah Smith serves it, and spends eight months in 1769–70 raising support for it in the South.
step: 1767 | The Warren Association unites New England Baptist churches and, in time, carries their grievances to the courts and Congress.
step: 1765–1805 | Smith pastors the First Baptist Church of Haverhill, Massachusetts, and serves as a chaplain in the Revolution from 1775 to 1780.

--- Daniel Merrill
lead: A Congregational pastor in Sedgwick, Maine, and a convinced pedobaptist, who studied the Bible on baptism when Baptists came to town in 1802.
step: 1805 | His church votes unanimously to become Baptist, and Merrill, his wife and sixty-four others are baptized in the Benjamin River.
step: 1808 | Writes *The Kingdom of Heaven Distinguished from Babylon*, holding that churches that admit the unbaptized are not baptized churches.
step: 1812 | *Miniature History of the Baptists*: their origin can be found “no where unless it be conceded that it was at Jordan or Enon.”
read: daniel-merrill | Read the full study: Daniel Merrill

=== The South
--- Pennsylvania and the associations
lead: Not every colony fought. Rhode Island and Pennsylvania offered liberty of conscience from the start, and Baptists there built the first networks.
step: 1682 | William Penn’s Pennsylvania grants liberty of conscience, and Baptists gather in and around Philadelphia.
step: 1707 | The Philadelphia Baptist Association forms, the first in America, and for generations helps the scattered churches stay together.
step: 1758 | The Sandy Creek Association forms in North Carolina, and Separate Baptists across the South join it.
step: 1767 | The Warren Association unites the New England churches, and by the 1770s it keeps a grievance committee to carry their cases.
note: The associations are why the Baptists could speak with one voice, and why Ford, Backus and Leland were sent as the agents of many churches, not as lone men.

--- Separate Baptists
lead: The Great Awakening reached the South through Separate Baptists, preachers called to preach whether the Anglican parish permitted it or not.
step: 1755 | **Shubal Stearns**, a Baptist since 1751, leaves Connecticut and plants the Sandy Creek church in North Carolina on November 22, with sixteen members. From it the Separate Baptists spread into Virginia, the Carolinas and Georgia.
step: c. 1770 | **Daniel Marshall**, Stearns’s brother-in-law, carries the work to Georgia, where he is arrested in St. Paul’s Parish and ordered to stop preaching. He preaches on, and settles at Kiokee in January 1771, where the church is constituted in 1772. [[Acts 4:19]]
p: Their preaching was warm, their meetings were long, and the parish clergy did not like it. They preached under open sky and in houses because no one would give them a pulpit.

--- Jailed for preaching
step: June 1768 | **Spotsylvania County.** John Waller, Lewis Craig, James Childs and others are seized and arraigned as “disturbers of the peace”. A lawyer complained they could not meet a man on the road without ramming a text of Scripture down his throat. They refused to promise to stop preaching, and lay forty-three days in jail, preaching through the grates.
step: 1768–70s | Baptist preachers are jailed in Spotsylvania, Caroline, Chesterfield, Culpeper and Orange counties. Again and again they preach through the jail grates to crowds outside.
step: 1770s | **Lewis Craig** and **Elijah Craig**, both jailed (Elijah in Culpeper and Orange counties), later carry their congregations to Kentucky.
callout: Patrick Henry, the Virginia patriot, is often pictured pleading for these jailed preachers. The speech credited to him is apocryphal, written later by a minister, according to Semple’s editor. But Henry did act as counsel for some imprisoned Baptist preachers in Caroline County, and Semple calls him a friend in their fight for licensed places to preach. Years later he backed a general tax for religion, and the Baptists opposed him (see “Multiple establishment”).

--- Reuben Ford
lead: Born about 1742, Ford was awakened under George Whitefield at about twenty, and baptized some seven years later after hearing the Baptist preachers Read and Harriss.
step: 1771 | He gathers the Goochland church, with about seventy-five members, among the earliest Baptist churches in Virginia, and pastors it for the rest of his ministry. Dover follows in 1773.
step: 30+ years | Clerk of the Dover Association.
step: Aug. 1785 | Clerk of the General Committee at Du Puy’s meetinghouse, Powhatan County, which resolved against the assessment bill. The committee appointed him to carry its remonstrance and petition to the General Assembly.
step: 1786–87 | Reports that the assessment did not pass, and that Jefferson’s act on religious liberty passed instead. He and John Leland then served as the committee’s agents against the Episcopal incorporation act.
p: Not a brilliant preacher, wrote his biographer, but his simplicity, affection and a blameless life won the confidence of everyone who knew him.
note: Sources: James B. Taylor, *Virginia Baptist Ministers* (1859); Robert B. Semple, *A History of the Rise and Progress of the Baptists in Virginia* (1810). Ford was the clerk and messenger of the General Committee, not the author of its resolution.

--- The Anglican establishment
panel-warn: Preaching licenses | Granted by the parish and the county court, and withheld from dissenters.
panel-warn: Parish taxes | Everyone paid the Church of England’s tithe, Baptists included.
panel-warn: Legal penalties for dissenters | County courts, parish vestries and crown-appointed governors enforced it all.
note: Not always one famous name, but a whole system of establishment.

=== The other camps
--- The establishment side
lead: They were not always villains. Many believed society could not hold together without the state’s support of religion.
panel-warn: John Cotton | Defended a close church-state relationship in early Massachusetts, and answered Roger Williams.
panel-warn: Increase and Cotton Mather | Upheld established church structures and the ideal of a godly commonwealth.
panel-warn: The Anglican system | Parish vestries, county courts and crown-appointed governors enforced the Church of England in the South.
callout: Their motives were order and morality. The Baptists’ answer was that Christ is the only head of the church, and conscience cannot be commanded. [[Colossians 1:18]]

--- Multiple establishment
lead: Patrick Henry wanted no single favored church, but he held that a moral republic could not stand without the state’s support of religion.
step: 1784 | The *Bill Establishing a Provision for Teachers of the Christian Religion* would tax every Virginian, and each taxpayer would name the Christian church that received his money. If he named none, it went to schools.
step: 1780 | Massachusetts’ constitution had already done the same, letting towns tax for “public Protestant teachers”, each taxpayer’s money going to his own denomination. Isaac Backus and the Baptists contested it.
quote: Whereas the general diffusion of Christian knowledge hath a natural tendency to correct the morals of men, restrain their vices, and preserve the peace of society… | Preamble to Henry’s 1784 bill
note: Henry’s case is civic, not gospel: Christian teaching is valued for good order. Scholars compare it to a public good that taxes should fund, like roads. That comparison is theirs, not Henry’s words.
callout: This was the harder argument for the Baptists, because it sounded fair. Their answer: a tax for religion, even an even-handed one, makes the state the judge of what is a church. [[Romans 14:4]]

--- The middle way: John Jay
lead: The middle way sits left of Henry. Jay rejected a national church and a single state denomination, yet he believed civil order and liberty were tied to Christian morality, and that the states could shape their laws to keep that heritage.
step: 1777 | At New York’s constitutional convention he moved to bar Roman Catholics from land and civil rights unless they renounced the Pope. That failed, but his naturalization oath, renouncing foreign ecclesiastical as well as civil authority, was adopted and stood until 1806.
step: 1795–1801 | As governor he followed New England precedent in proclaiming days of Thanksgiving, and pressed for laws requiring observance of the Sabbath, the “blue laws” that closed work, trade and amusement on Sunday.
step: 1816 | Writes to Jedidiah Morse that “it is the duty as well as the privilege and interest of our Christian nation to select and prefer Christians for their rulers.”
note: Sources: John R. Vile, “John Jay,” First Amendment Encyclopedia (Middle Tennessee State University); on blue laws, The New York Times, Oct. 7, 1963, “The Archaic Blue Laws.”
callout: This is the middle way in a single man. No state church, but a state that leans on Christianity and limits the civil standing of those outside it. The Baptists’ soul liberty asked more: equal liberty for every conscience. [[Galatians 5:1]]


=== The turning point
--- Baptists and the Revolution
lead: Virginia’s Baptists had been ground under the established church, and Semple writes that they were “to a man” favorable to any revolution that would give them freedom of religion.
step: Aug. 1775 | At Du Puy’s meetinghouse in Powhatan County, the united Baptist body resolves to petition the Virginia Convention to abolish the church establishment and to leave religion to stand on its own merits.
step: Aug. 1775 | It also asks leave to preach to the army, which is granted. Jeremiah Walker and John Williams preach to the soldiers, but find little encouragement and drop it.
step: 1775–80 | In New England, Hezekiah Smith serves as an army chaplain.
note: Mere toleration was not enough, Semple notes, since they had been jailed even where the toleration law was thought to be in force.

--- Virginia, 1776–1786
step: 1776 | The Declaration of Rights, Article 16, guarantees “free exercise” of religion. James Madison’s amendment replaced Mason’s “toleration” with “free exercise”.
step: 1784 | Patrick Henry’s general assessment bill would have taxed every Virginian for the church of his choice. Baptists petition against it through their General Committee.
step: 1785 | James Madison’s *Memorial and Remonstrance* helps defeat it.
step: 1786 | The Virginia Statute for Religious Freedom, drafted by Jefferson, passes.

--- Madison answers Henry
lead: Madison’s *Memorial and Remonstrance* (1785) took Henry’s civic argument head on.
quote: The Bill implies either that the Civil Magistrate is a competent Judge of Religious Truth; or that he may employ Religion as an engine of Civil policy. The first is an arrogant pretension falsified by the contradictory opinions of Rulers in all ages, and throughout the world: the second an unhallowed perversion of the means of salvation. | James Madison, *Memorial and Remonstrance*, section 5
quote: During almost fifteen centuries has the legal establishment of Christianity been on trial. What have been its fruits? More or less in all places, pride and indolence in the Clergy, ignorance and servility in the laity, in both, superstition, bigotry and persecution. | James Madison, *Memorial and Remonstrance*, section 7
note: Madison argued from the good of the state. The Baptists argued from the gospel, as the slide “Different roots, same conclusion” shows.

--- The General Committee resolves
lead: On August 18, 1785, the Baptist General Committee met at Du Puy’s meetinghouse in Powhatan County, with William Webber moderator and Reuben Ford clerk. Semple records its resolution.
quote: …it is believed to be repugnant to the spirit of the Gospel for the Legislature thus to proceed in matters of religion; that no human laws ought to be established for this purpose; but that every person ought to be left entirely free in respect to matters of religion; that the holy Author of our religion needs no such compulsive measures for the promotion of His cause; that the Gospel wants not the feeble arm of man for its support; that it has made, and will again through divine power, make its way against all opposition; and that should the Legislature assume the right of taxing the people for the support of the Gospel, it will be destructive to religious liberty. | Baptist General Committee, August 1785, in Semple’s *History of the Baptists in Virginia*
p: It urged every county that had not yet petitioned to do so against the engrossed bill, and appointed Reuben Ford to carry its remonstrance to the General Assembly. [[Matthew 16:18]] [[Zechariah 4:6]]
note: Semple’s own judgment: the Baptists, he believed, were the only sect who plainly remonstrated, and the bill’s defeat owed much to them. Not all histories would put it that strongly.

--- The petitions
lead: Madison’s essay was not the only answer. In the fall of 1785 a printed petition went through central and southern Virginia, and far more people signed it than signed the *Remonstrance*.
quote: …that they are inwardly moved by the Holy Ghost to take upon them that Office, that they seek the good of Mankind and not worldly Interest. Let their doctrines be scriptural and their Lives upright. Then shall Religion (if departed) speedily return, and Deism be put to open shame… | Virginia petition against the general assessment, 1785
p: Its answer to Henry was the gospel’s own: ministers are called by God, and the church needs no tax collector to keep it. Copies went to the General Assembly from many counties. [[Acts 20:28]] [[1 Peter 5:2]]
note: Source: quoted in The Heritage Foundation, “Did America Have a Christian Founding?”. The original forms are in the Legislative Petitions collection at the Library of Virginia. Whether Baptists wrote this one is not yet confirmed.

--- John Leland
step: 1754–1841 | Ordained in Virginia in 1775, Leland pastored there until he went home to Massachusetts in 1791.
step: 1788 | Tradition holds that Leland, who opposed the Constitution for lacking a bill of rights, met Madison in Orange County in March and stood aside for his election to the Virginia ratifying convention, on an understanding that Madison would seek one. The details vary by source, and Madison’s own public stance was more guarded.
step: 1791 | *The Rights of Conscience Inalienable*: liberty for all men, “Pagan, Turk, Jew or Christian”, not for Baptists alone.
p: Leland’s conviction was the Baptist one: the same freedom he asked for himself, he asked for those he disagreed with. [[Matthew 7:12]]

--- Different roots, same conclusion
lead: In Virginia the Baptists found allies in Jefferson and Madison, who wanted the state out of religion for other reasons.
panel: The Enlightenment wing | Jefferson and Madison reasoned from natural rights: religion is a private matter, and government has no jurisdiction over it.
panel: The Baptists | They reasoned from the gospel: faith must be free, because a faith that cannot be refused means nothing. [[John 6:44]] [[Acts 8:36-37]]
callout: Both pressed past the middle way, where the state still leans on Christianity through taxes, oaths and laws. The Baptists did not become secular for it. They asked the state to leave the church alone, so that the church would be free to be the church. [[John 18:36]]

--- The First Amendment
big: Galatians 5:1 | mark: Stand fast therefore in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free
p: In 1791 the First Amendment was ratified: no establishment of religion and no prohibiting the free exercise of it. The Baptist voices of Backus, Leland and the Virginia committee were among those that carried the argument.
callout: In 1801 the Danbury Baptists wrote to President Jefferson, since Connecticut still had a state church. His 1802 reply spoke of a “wall of separation.” In their case it is a wall to keep the state out of the church, not the church out of society.

--- Disestablishment, state by state
step: 1786 | Virginia ends its establishment.
step: 1818 | Connecticut ends tax support for its Congregational church.
step: 1819 | New Hampshire ends its religious tax (some sources give 1817 or 1818).
step: 1833 | Massachusetts is the last state to disestablish, almost two centuries after Holmes was whipped.
note: The First Amendment bound only the national government at first. The states had to follow one at a time.

=== What it means
--- The thread
lead: Holmes, Merrill, Backus, Leland and the rest are one thread.
- Willing to suffer for believer’s baptism.
- Unwilling to submit to state church authority.
- Strong for religious liberty for everyone, even those they disagreed with.
- Faith was a matter between a man and God, not a matter of law. [[1 Timothy 2:5]]
refs: Hebrews 11:36-38; 2 Timothy 3:12

--- A line of believers
panel: The Trail of Blood | These men stand in the line of believers who held the same distinctives and paid for them in every age. [[Hebrews 12:1]]
panel: Our inheritance | Soul liberty, the local church and believer’s baptism are not American inventions, but Bible convictions people bled to keep. [[Acts 5:29]]
read: doctrine-week-5-trail-of-blood-and-baptist-history | Week 5: A Trail of Blood
read: baptist-perpetuity | Baptist Perpetuity

=== Handle with care
--- Handle with care
panel: The phrase | “A wall of separation between church and state” is from Jefferson’s 1802 letter to the Danbury Baptists, not from the First Amendment. For the Baptists, the wall kept the state out of the church.
panel: The sources | Quote the primary text, not the summary. Popular quotes get garbled, which is why this lesson quotes Semple, Madison and Henry’s own words.
panel: The limits | Soul liberty and the local church are Bible convictions. This history shows men who bled for them, not a system of succession. [[Acts 17:11]]

--- Where to read more
step: Primary | Robert B. Semple, *A History of the Rise and Progress of the Baptists in Virginia* (1810). Isaac Backus, *An Appeal to the Public for Religious Liberty* (1773). John Leland, *The Rights of Conscience Inalienable* (1791). James Madison, *Memorial and Remonstrance* (1785). Thomas Helwys, *A Short Declaration of the Mystery of Iniquity* (1612).
step: Lives | James B. Taylor, *Virginia Baptist Ministers* (1859). David Benedict, *A General History of the Baptist Denomination*.
step: Reference | John R. Vile, “John Jay,” First Amendment Encyclopedia (MTSU). Encyclopedia Virginia and the Dictionary of Virginia Biography (Elijah and Lewis Craig).
